% 38e-runtime-linux.weft — the Linux/x86-64 OS layer for the second target.

\chapter{The Linux Runtime}
\label{ch:runtime-linux}

Chapter~\ref{ch:syscalls} gave the compiler its ARM64/macOS floor: thin
wrappers over \texttt{svc \#0x80} and a Mach-O entry point.  Everything above
that floor is target-neutral by construction --- the ported subsystems bottom
out on the same \texttt{kcc\_sys\_*} names --- so bringing \kcc{} to a second
operating system is a matter of laying a \emph{second} floor with the same
shape.  This chapter is that floor for Linux on x86-64: the ELF entry point and
the syscall wrappers, hand-written in the assembly the platform's \texttt{as}
consumes, so that a translation unit \kcc{} emits with \texttt{--target=x86\_64-linux}
links into a running executable with no C library at all.

It is a distinct artefact from the compiler's own object files: it is never part
of the ARM64 \kcc{} binary, and it is assembled on a Linux host (or by a cross
assembler).  It is the runtime that the compiler's Linux output --- and,
once every subsystem is C, the Linux \kcc{} itself --- links against.

\section{Calling Convention and Syscall ABI}

The Linux/x86-64 syscall ABI differs from macOS/ARM64 in three ways that the
wrappers must respect.  The syscall \emph{number} goes in \reg{rax} (not a
dedicated register), the trap is the \texttt{syscall} instruction (not
\texttt{svc}), and the fourth argument travels in \reg{r10} --- because the
System~V C convention puts it in \reg{rcx}, but \texttt{syscall} clobbers
\reg{rcx} (and \reg{r11}) as part of its operation.  So a wrapper that forwards
four or more C arguments must copy \reg{rcx} into \reg{r10} first.  The result
comes back in \reg{rax}, with errors as small negative values.

@d Runtime-linux: constants @{
# Linux/x86-64 syscall numbers (arch/x86/entry/syscalls/syscall_64.tbl)
.set SYS_read,     0
.set SYS_write,    1
.set SYS_open,     2
.set SYS_close,    3
.set SYS_lseek,    8
.set SYS_mmap,     9
.set SYS_munmap,  11
.set SYS_fork,    57
.set SYS_execve,  59
.set SYS_exit,    60
.set SYS_wait4,   61
.set SYS_unlink,  87
@}

\section{The ELF Entry Point}

The kernel transfers control to \texttt{\_start} with the stack, not registers,
carrying the program arguments: \reg{rsp} points at \texttt{argc}, immediately
followed by the \texttt{argv} pointers, a null terminator, and then the
\texttt{envp} pointers.  There is no return address and no C runtime to unwind
into.  The entry code reconstructs the System~V argument registers a C
\texttt{main} expects --- \texttt{argc} in \reg{rdi}, \texttt{argv} in
\reg{rsi}, \texttt{envp} in \reg{rdx} --- calls \texttt{main}, and turns its
return value into an \texttt{exit} syscall.  Since \reg{rsp} is sixteen-byte
aligned at process entry and the \texttt{call} pushes the eight-byte return
address, \texttt{main} sees exactly the alignment the ABI promises.

@d Runtime-linux: start @{
	.text
	.globl _start
_start:
	# Run the module initializers before main.  kcc lowers a non-main
	# translation unit's file-scope initializers into a synthesised
	# `kcc.module_init` and registers its address in .init_array (the ELF form
	# of a Mach-O __mod_init_func); a normal C runtime runs those before main,
	# but a -nostdlib static executable has none, so the entry does it here.
	# Without this, every non-main TU's initialised global keeps its zero image
	# --- e.g. io's `kcc_io_fd = 1` stays 0, so buffered output goes to fd 0.
	# The linker provides __init_array_start/_end around the section; %rbx and
	# %rbp are callee-saved, so they survive each initializer call, and the
	# balanced call/ret keeps %rsp (hence argc at (%rsp)) unchanged.
	leaq	__init_array_start(%rip), %rbx
	leaq	__init_array_end(%rip), %rbp
.Linit_loop:
	cmpq	%rbp, %rbx
	jae	.Linit_done
	call	*(%rbx)
	addq	$8, %rbx
	jmp	.Linit_loop
.Linit_done:
	movq	(%rsp), %rdi           # argc
	leaq	8(%rsp), %rsi          # argv  = &stack[1]
	leaq	16(%rsp,%rdi,8), %rdx  # envp  = &argv[argc+1]
	call	main
	movq	%rax, %rdi             # exit status = main()'s return value
	movq	$SYS_exit, %rax
	syscall
	hlt                            # unreachable
@}

\section{Process Control}

\texttt{exit} terminates the process and never returns; \texttt{fork},
\texttt{execve}, and \texttt{wait4} are the three the driver uses to run the
assembler and linker as child processes.  Each is a direct forward of its C
arguments to the syscall, with the \reg{rcx}$\rightarrow$\reg{r10} fix-up where a
fourth argument is present.

@d Runtime-linux: process @{
# void kcc_sys_exit(int status)          — %edi = status; does not return.
	.globl kcc_sys_exit
kcc_sys_exit:
	movq	$SYS_exit, %rax
	syscall
	hlt

# long kcc_sys_fork(void)
	.globl kcc_sys_fork
kcc_sys_fork:
	movq	$SYS_fork, %rax
	syscall
	ret

# long kcc_sys_execve(const char *path, char *const argv[], char *const envp[])
	.globl kcc_sys_execve
kcc_sys_execve:
	movq	$SYS_execve, %rax
	syscall
	ret

# long kcc_sys_wait4(long pid, int *status, int options, void *rusage)
	.globl kcc_sys_wait4
kcc_sys_wait4:
	movq	%rcx, %r10             # 4th arg: SysV %rcx -> syscall %r10
	movq	$SYS_wait4, %rax
	syscall
	ret
@}

\section{File I/O}

The file primitives --- \texttt{read}, \texttt{write}, \texttt{open},
\texttt{close}, \texttt{lseek}, \texttt{unlink} --- each take at most three
arguments, so the System~V registers \reg{rdi}, \reg{rsi}, \reg{rdx} already sit
where \texttt{syscall} wants them; the wrappers only set the number and trap.

@d Runtime-linux: fileio @{
# long kcc_sys_read(long fd, void *buf, long len)
	.globl kcc_sys_read
kcc_sys_read:
	movq	$SYS_read, %rax
	syscall
	ret

# long kcc_sys_write(long fd, const void *buf, long len)
	.globl kcc_sys_write
kcc_sys_write:
	movq	$SYS_write, %rax
	syscall
	ret

# long kcc_sys_open(const char *path, long flags, long mode)
# kcc hard-codes the macOS open() flag values (O_CREAT=0x200, O_TRUNC=0x400),
# so when it runs on Linux those bits mean something else and an output file is
# never created.  Translate the two flag words kcc actually emits --- 0 (read)
# and 0x601 (write|create|trunc) --- into the Linux convention (O_CREAT=0x40,
# O_TRUNC=0x200) before the syscall; the access mode (low two bits) is identical.
	.globl kcc_sys_open
kcc_sys_open:
	movq	%rsi, %r11             # macOS-convention flags
	andq	$3, %rsi               # keep access mode (RDONLY/WRONLY/RDWR)
	testq	$0x200, %r11           # macOS O_CREAT
	jz	1f
	orq	$0x40, %rsi            # Linux O_CREAT
1:	testq	$0x400, %r11           # macOS O_TRUNC
	jz	2f
	orq	$0x200, %rsi           # Linux O_TRUNC
2:	movq	$SYS_open, %rax
	syscall
	ret

# long kcc_sys_close(long fd)
	.globl kcc_sys_close
kcc_sys_close:
	movq	$SYS_close, %rax
	syscall
	ret

# long kcc_sys_lseek(long fd, long off, long whence)
	.globl kcc_sys_lseek
kcc_sys_lseek:
	movq	$SYS_lseek, %rax
	syscall
	ret

# long kcc_sys_unlink(const char *path)
	.globl kcc_sys_unlink
kcc_sys_unlink:
	movq	$SYS_unlink, %rax
	syscall
	ret
@}

\section{Memory Mapping}

\texttt{mmap} is where the two operating systems disagree on more than
registers.  The arena (Chapter~\ref{ch:self-hosting-arena}) requests its chunks
with \texttt{flags = MAP\_PRIVATE | MAP\_ANON}, but that constant is
\texttt{0x1002} on macOS and \texttt{0x22} on Linux --- the anonymous bit is
\texttt{0x1000} there and \texttt{0x20} here.  Rather than fork the arena for
each target, the OS layer owns the difference: when the incoming flags carry the
Mach anonymous bit, the wrapper clears it and sets the Linux one, so the arena's
unmodified \texttt{kcc\_sys\_mmap(0, size, 3, 0x1002, -1, 0)} maps anonymous
private memory here too.  (A future refactor may replace the raw \texttt{mmap}
call with a target-neutral ``map anonymous'' primitive; until then this
translation is the seam.)  Beyond the flags, \texttt{mmap} is a six-argument
syscall, so the fourth argument still takes the \reg{rcx}$\rightarrow$\reg{r10}
route.

@d Runtime-linux: mmap @{
# void *kcc_sys_mmap(void *addr, long len, int prot, int flags, int fd, long off)
	.globl kcc_sys_mmap
kcc_sys_mmap:
	testq	$0x1000, %rcx          # Mach MAP_ANON present?
	jz	.Lmmap_go
	btrq	$12, %rcx              # clear Mach MAP_ANON (0x1000)
	orq	$0x20, %rcx            # set Linux MAP_ANONYMOUS
.Lmmap_go:
	movq	%rcx, %r10             # 4th arg: SysV %rcx -> syscall %r10
	movq	$SYS_mmap, %rax
	syscall
	ret

# long kcc_sys_munmap(void *addr, long len)
	.globl kcc_sys_munmap
kcc_sys_munmap:
	movq	$SYS_munmap, %rax
	syscall
	ret
@}

\section{Assembling the Runtime}

The runtime is a single \texttt{.s} file.  Marking the stack non-executable
keeps the linker from emitting an executable-stack warning or an \texttt{RWX}
segment.

@d Runtime-linux: file @{
@<Runtime-linux: constants@>
@<Runtime-linux: start@>
@<Runtime-linux: process@>
@<Runtime-linux: fileio@>
@<Runtime-linux: mmap@>
	.section .note.GNU-stack,"",@@progbits
@}

\section{Verification: A Cross-Compiled, Native Linux Run}
\label{sec:meta-a-verify}

\texttt{make meta-a} closes the loop the retargeting playbook set out: it takes a
small \emph{multi-file} C program, compiles each translation unit with this
compiler under \texttt{--target=x86\_64-linux}, links the results against the
runtime above --- statically, with no libc --- and runs the executable on Linux.
The program exercises the three seams the runtime provides: it maps arena memory
through \texttt{kcc\_sys\_mmap}, calls across a translation-unit boundary, writes
its result through \texttt{kcc\_sys\_write}, and returns a status the harness
checks.  A green run means a program \kcc{} compiled runs natively on a
different operating system and architecture than the one \kcc{} runs on.

\paragraph{The program under test.}  Two translation units, to prove
cross-object linking: \texttt{demo\_util.c} provides a leaf routine, and
\texttt{demo\_main.c} drives the arena and I/O.

@d Runtime-linux: demo util @{
/* demo_util.c — a leaf called across a TU boundary. */
long demo_sum(const long *a, long n) {
    long s = 0, i = 0;
    while (i < n) { s = s + a[i]; i = i + 1; }
    return s;
}
@}

@d Runtime-linux: demo main @{
/* demo_main.c — arena via mmap, cross-TU call, output, distinctive exit. */
extern long kcc_sys_write(long fd, const void *buf, long len);
extern void *kcc_sys_mmap(void *addr, long len, int prot, int flags,
                          int fd, long off);
extern long demo_sum(const long *a, long n);

static unsigned long slen(const char *s) {
    unsigned long n = 0;
    while (s[n]) n++;
    return n;
}

int main(void) {
    /* 0x1002 = the arena's macOS MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANON; the runtime translates it. */
    long *p = (long *)kcc_sys_mmap(0, 1 << 16, 3, 0x1002, -1, 0);
    if ((long)p == -1 || p == 0) return 1;

    long i = 0;
    while (i < 6) { p[i] = i + 1; i = i + 1; }   /* 1+2+3+4+5+6 = 21 */

    const char *msg = "kcc cross-compiled to linux/x86-64, running native\n";
    kcc_sys_write(1, msg, slen(msg));

    long got = demo_sum(p, 6) + demo_sum(p, 6);   /* 21 + 21 = 42 */
    return (int)got;
}
@}

@d Runtime-linux: meta-a verify @{
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# make meta-a — cross-compile a multi-TU C program to Linux/x86-64 with kcc,
# link it against the Linux runtime (no libc), and run it natively.
#
# kcc runs on the (ARM64/macOS) build host and emits ELF-flavour .s; the
# assemble/link/run happens on Linux, here inside a linux/amd64 container.
# Apple Silicon's Docker uses Rosetta, whose ELF loader rejects a valid
# non-page-aligned RW segment ("bss_size overflow"); we run under qemu-x86_64,
# a conforming loader, which is what a real Linux kernel behaves like.
set -euo pipefail

R=tmp/runtime
KCC=tmp/kcc

echo "[1/3] kcc compiles the demo TUs -> Linux/x86-64 .s"
"$KCC" -c "$R/demo_util.c" -o "$R/demo_util.s" --target=x86_64-linux >/dev/null
"$KCC" -c "$R/demo_main.c" -o "$R/demo_main.s" --target=x86_64-linux >/dev/null

echo "[2/3] assemble + link + run inside linux/amd64 (qemu loader)"
docker run --rm -v "$PWD/$R":/w -w /w --platform linux/amd64 gcc:latest bash -c '
  set -e
  apt-get update -qq >/dev/null 2>&1 && apt-get install -y -qq qemu-user >/dev/null 2>&1
  as --64 os_linux.s   -o os_linux.o
  as --64 demo_util.s  -o demo_util.o
  as --64 demo_main.s  -o demo_main.o
  ld -static -nostdlib -e _start -o demo os_linux.o demo_util.o demo_main.o
  file demo
  set +e; qemu-x86_64 ./demo; code=$?; set -e
  echo "exit=$code (expect 42)"
  [ "$code" -eq 42 ] && echo "META_A_PASS" || { echo "META_A_FAIL"; exit 1; }
'

echo "[3/3] done"
@}

\section{kcc Self-Hosts on Linux}
\label{sec:meta-c}

The runtime above lets a program \kcc{} compiled run on Linux; the natural
question is whether \emph{\kcc{} itself} is such a program.  It is.  The
milestone ladder has three rungs, each strictly stronger than the last.
\emph{Meta-A} (\S\ref{sec:meta-a-verify}) shows \kcc{}'s \emph{output} runs on
Linux.  \emph{Meta-B} shows \kcc{} \emph{runs} on Linux: cross-compile every
one of \kcc{}'s now-all-C subsystems under \texttt{--target=x86\_64-linux},
assemble and statically link them with the runtime and no libc, and the
resulting ELF lexes, parses, checks and compiles C under \texttt{qemu-x86\_64}.
\emph{Meta-C}, this section, shows \kcc{} \emph{self-hosts} on Linux: that Linux
binary, call it \texttt{kcc\_linux}, compiles \kcc{}'s own source into a
second-generation compiler that is itself correct.

The bootstrap is deliberately simple.  \texttt{kcc\_linux} compiles each of its
twenty-nine C modules (the lexer slot is the compiler's real lexer, not the
standalone tokenizer) under \texttt{--target=x86\_64-linux}; the host
\texttt{as} and \texttt{ld} assemble and link them, again static and libc-free,
into \texttt{kcc\_linux2}.  The second generation compiles \texttt{hello.c} to
exit~42, a floating-point program to its expected value --- exercising the SSE
rung the \texttt{x86-64} back end now carries in C --- and a character constant
to its true code.  Crucially, \texttt{kcc\_linux} and \texttt{kcc\_linux2} emit
\emph{byte-identical} assembly for every module: the compiler compiled by itself
reproduces itself.  That is the fixed point that distinguishes a self-hosting
compiler from one that merely runs.

Reaching the fixed point surfaced one genuine bug and one cosmetic one, both
invisible until the whole compiler was \kcc{}-compiled \emph{for} \texttt{x86-64}
rather than \texttt{arm64}.  The genuine bug was a small-struct return-ABI
mismatch in the character-literal path (\S on primary expressions): a two-word
\texttt{struct} received through a \texttt{void~*} declaration, which reads the
first return register.  On \texttt{arm64} that register holds the first field;
the \texttt{x86-64} back end returns such a struct as a pointer to it, so the
character's value became the low byte of an address.  Receiving the real struct
fixes it under either convention.  The cosmetic one: a stray space byte lands in
the \texttt{s\_globl} directive string at runtime, so the self-compiled compiler
writes \texttt{.globl}~with two spaces before each name.  The assembler accepts
it and the output assembles, links and runs --- and because the quirk is
deterministic it reproduces exactly in both generations, so the fixed point
still holds --- but it is a real miscompiled address computation left for a
later pass.

Because the bootstrap needs an \texttt{x86-64} Linux toolchain and a conforming
ELF loader --- a \texttt{linux/amd64} container plus \texttt{qemu-x86\_64} on an
Apple-Silicon host --- it runs as a manual procedure rather than a \texttt{make}
target, in the spirit of the Meta-A verifier above but one rung higher.
